  "reasoning": "The patient reports calf cramps, difficulty walking, and feeling tired after working, while noting obesity as a potential cause. Analyzing the symptom cluster: obesity increases pressure on the lower extremity veins, making it a primary risk factor for varicose veins. Calf cramps, leg heaviness, and fatigue/tiredness after standing or working are classic clinical presentations of venous insufficiency and varicose veins. Comparing against other options: arthritis involves joint inflammation rather than isolated calf cramps; diabetes presents with metabolic signs like excessive thirst and urination; hypertension is often asymptomatic; cervical spondylosis affects the neck; and respiratory or infectious diseases (asthma, malaria, dengue, etc.) present with fever, cough, or rash. Training references [6], [9], and [12] directly correlate leg pain, regular cramps, and difficulty running/walking with varicose veins. Given the precise match of symptoms and risk factors, varicose veins is the definitive diagnosis.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]varicose veins[/DIAGNOSIS]"
